      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hmm, but the host kernel\u0027s `pte_mkold()` clears it non-atomically?\n\n```\nstatic inline pte_t pte_mkold(pte_t pte)\n{\n\treturn pte_clear_flags(pte, _PAGE_ACCESSED);\n}\n```\n```\nstatic inline pte_t pte_clear_flags(pte_t pte, pteval_t clear)\n{\n\tpteval_t v \u003d native_pte_val(pte);\n\n\treturn native_make_pte(v \u0026 ~clear);\n}\n```\n\n(Although that is for MMU, not for EPT, but that shouldn\u0027t be fundamentally different?)\n\nI see KVM\u0027s TDP MMU does clear it atomically (in `kvm_tdp_mmu_age_spte()`), but the reason for that is just to be able to do that without locking kvm-\u003emmu_lock: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20250204004038.1680123-5-jthoughton@google.com/ (looks like before that patch it was clearing it non-atomically)?\n\nIt seems to me that if hardware sets this bit between the read and the write-back, the only \"bad\" thing that happens is that the write-back unintentionally clears this freshly set bit; but it would clear it anyway if the hardware set it slightly earlier, before the read? So what\u0027s the actual problem?\n\nWhy would it lead to a data corruption?",
      "revId": "4d39346dee7059bcc8dc59684e16a6a7906390b8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
