      "message": "\u003e But in our case \"software\" means the pKVM hypervisor, and the pKVM hypervisor doesn\u0027t seem to actually use the dirty bit in EPT PTEs? So basically we don\u0027t need to care if we clobber it?\n\nYes, that is true. But since the fix is simple and no drawback, maybe it is better to fix this to avoid potential issues in the future if the dirty bit is used, e.g., supporting migation?\n\n\u003e As for the host kernel, I imagine it may actually rely on the dirty bit in MMU PTEs. And yet, pte_mkold() is implemented non-atomically. Is it because no one has ever noticed this issue in Linux kernel through all these years, or perhaps kernel doesn\u0027t actually rely on the dirty bit in such a way that this would cause any serious issues? I\u0027m not a MM expert...\n\nJust saw another function `ptep_test_and_clear_young()` in the `arch/x86/mm/pgtable.c` to automatically clear the access bit without clobbering the dirty bits. Maybe this one is used for a live pte, while `pte_mkold` is used build a pte which is not active yet?",
